<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # My Family and I – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> My Family and I</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> She Is My Mom!</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b><br>
            Mediation: Text: Use pictures or objects to express ideas.<br>
            Communication: Use gestures and demonstrations to help classmates perform a simple task.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b><br>
            Mediation: Can use pictures and gestures to describe their family to classmates (e.g., miming or displaying a drawing or picture of a family member).<br>
            Can clarify responses by pointing to family pictures or objects (e.g., pointing to a picture of their mother).<br>
            Can use simple gestures or facial expressions to show who they are talking about in their family (e.g., pointing to a family member's picture).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f0f8ff;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher holds up flashcards of family members (mom, dad, brother, sister, grandmother, grandfather) one by one. Students choral repeat the names. The teacher then quickly flashes them and asks, "Who is she/he?" Students respond with "Mom!" or "Dad!" The teacher praises correct answers and focuses on /m/ and /d/ sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher shows a picture of her own family. She points to each person and says, "This is my mom. She is friendly." (smiles and points to her mom in the picture). "This is my dad. He is tall." (gestures height and points to her dad). She uses simple gestures for adjectives (friendly, tall) and points clearly to each person while speaking.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to mom in the picture) "Is she my mom?" (Students: "Yes!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to dad) "Is he my sister?" (Students: "No!") "Who is he?" (Students: "Dad!")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher introduces the "Family Member Interview" project that students started at home. She explains that today students will share their family members using the pictures they brought.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher's own set of simple family drawings/pictures (similar to what students created), representing the "interview results."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Introducing family members using simple sentences to describe family (e.g., "She is my mom.") and clarifying responses by pointing to family pictures or objects.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Action:</b> The teacher models how to present the project. She holds up a drawing of her 'mom' and says, "This is my mom. I asked her, 'Who is she?' She said, 'She is your mom!'" (She points to the picture and then to herself, simplifying the 'interview' process for the 1st graders). She repeats for another family member: "This is my brother. He is young." (points to the picture, gestures 'young').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher shows her pictures again and asks, "Who is this?" (pointing to the 'mom' picture). Students respond, "Mom!" Then, "Is she old or young?" (Students respond: "Old!" or "Young!" based on the picture and previous description). The teacher uses gestures for old/young.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Using personal pronouns ("She is," "He is"), possessive adjectives ("my mom"), WH-questions ("Who is she/he?"), and basic family vocabulary with pictures and gestures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled practice (Picture-Sentence Matching):</b> Students take out their family pictures/drawings. The teacher provides sentence frames on the board or individual cards: "This is my ____." "She is ____." "He is ____." Students point to a picture and practice saying the sentence using the frame. (e.g., "This is my mom. She is friendly." "This is my dad. He is tall.") The teacher walks around and monitors pronunciation, especially /m/, /d/, /b/, /s/ sounds.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled (Pair Practice - Mini-Interview):</b> Students work in pairs. Student A holds up one of their family pictures. Student B asks, "Who is she/he?" Student A responds, "She is my mom!" or "He is my brother!" (using the vocabulary they collected). They switch roles. The teacher encourages them to use gestures (pointing to the picture) to clarify.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Clarification Practice):</b> In pairs, if one student is unsure of a word, their partner should try to help by pointing to the picture again, miming the action (e.g., hugging for 'love'), or repeating the word clearly. For example, if a student forgets "sister," the partner can point to the picture and say "sister!" with emphasis, using the /s/ sound.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small Group Presentation of "Family Member Interview" Project.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students form small groups (3-4 students). Each student takes a turn presenting their "Family Member Interview" project using their collected pictures/drawings. They "mediate" the information they gathered about their family.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each student holds up one family picture/drawing at a time.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They say: "This is my mom/dad/brother/sister." (using the proper personal pronoun and possessive adjective). They should point clearly to the picture.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They can add one simple descriptive word: "She is friendly." or "He is tall." (using gestures).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Classmates in the group can ask, "Who is he/she?" and the presenter responds, pointing to the correct picture.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reminds students to use gestures and pointing to help their classmates understand who they are talking about, practicing the mediation objective. They focus on clarifying their responses by pointing to the family pictures.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher circulates, offering support, prompting with "Who is she/he?" and encouraging collaboration.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist during group presentations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their "Family Member Interview" project to their small group, using their pictures and gestures to mediate the information.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can use pictures/drawings to present family members.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can point to pictures to clarify who they are talking about.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can use simple gestures/facial expressions to describe or clarify.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses "She is my mom" or "He is my dad" (or other family members).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation of family words is generally intelligible (e.g., /m/ in mom, /d/ in dad).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responds to "Who is she/he?" with an appropriate simple answer.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                Students complete a simple "exit ticket" by drawing a happy face if they used pictures to show their family and pointed, or a sad face if they found it hard.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did I use my pictures to show my family? (Draw a happy or sad face)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did I point to my mom/dad when I talked about them? (Draw a happy or sad face)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was easy about showing your family pictures and talking about them?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was hard about explaining your family to your friends?</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The project presentation allowed students to effectively mediate information using visuals and simple gestures. The 1st graders were engaged with showing their pictures. Some students needed more prompting for gestures, while others naturally used them. The pronunciation of /m/, /d/, /b/, /s/ sounds was generally good, showing good retention from previous lessons. The use of "Who is she/he?" and "She/He is my..." was appropriate for their level.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Adjustments for next time could include providing more structured sentence starters for introducing characteristics (e.g., "She is ____.") to encourage more descriptive language beyond just names. Also, dedicate a few more minutes to modeling non-verbal communication for clarification.</p>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "My Family and I" theme. The next theme will build on basic descriptions, possibly moving to describing pets or daily routines with family members.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>